# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Voskresenskaya Station -- the prologue. Thoughts, the toolkit, and the four
# ways out.
#
# The toolkit is deliberately real. Every tool in here does in the fiction what
# it does on a real engagement, including the parts that are inconvenient: Burp
# cannot read TLS until its CA is trusted, a capture is worthless without a
# span port or a hub, and a cloned card is a copy of somebody's actual day.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ===========================================================================
# THOUGHTS
# ===========================================================================

:: thought the_held_state
NAME       The Held State
PREMISE    The station did not crash. Crashing is loud and it is honest and it
PREMISE    takes everything down with it. This stopped. The lights are on, the
PREMISE    escalators are turning, the clock is keeping perfect time, and one
PREMISE    subsystem somewhere is refusing to advance and taking the whole
PREMISE    building hostage politely.
CONCLUSION You know the shape of it now. Somebody opened a transaction and
CONCLUSION never closed it. Every door in this station is waiting on a lock
CONCLUSION that a process is still holding, and that process is not stuck --
CONCLUSION it is being held. There is a hand on it. You have started looking
CONCLUSION for the hand instead of the fault, and you are not going to be able
CONCLUSION to stop.
BEATS      6
RESEARCH   Composure -1
BONUS      Logic 2
BONUS      Interfacing 1

:: thought glass_saints
NAME       The Saints Are A Status Page
PREMISE    The rose window above the concourse is nine metres of leaded glass
PREMISE    and it is also the departure board. The figures in the lancets are
PREMISE    not saints. They are services. When one goes amber, a line is
PREMISE    degraded. You have been staring up at it like a tourist and reading
PREMISE    it like an operator and the two are fighting.
CONCLUSION Somebody built a monitoring dashboard and made it beautiful enough
CONCLUSION that four hundred thousand people a day look up at it without once
CONCLUSION realising they are watching infrastructure fail in real time. You
CONCLUSION find that either the best or the worst thing anyone has ever done
CONCLUSION with stained glass, and you have stopped trying to decide which.
BEATS      4
BONUS      Perception 2
BONUS      Conceptualization 1

:: thought read_only_hands
NAME       Read-Only Hands
PREMISE    You have been in this station eleven minutes and you have already
PREMISE    put a capture on a network that did not invite you. You keep telling
PREMISE    yourself that listening is not touching. You have told yourself that
PREMISE    before, in other buildings, and you remember how those ended.
CONCLUSION There is no read-only. There is only the part where you have not
CONCLUSION written yet. Every capture is a decision you are deferring, and
CONCLUSION tonight you are going to stop deferring it, and the honest thing is
CONCLUSION to admit you knew that when you opened the bag.
BEATS      5
RESEARCH   Volition -1
BONUS      Interfacing 2
BONUS      Half Light 1

:: thought layer_two_animal
NAME       Layer Two Animal
PREMISE    You have started hearing the station as frames. The turnstiles are
PREMISE    chattering, the PA is unicast when it should be multicast, and
PREMISE    something on the maintenance VLAN is ARPing for a gateway that has
PREMISE    not answered since the doors closed.
CONCLUSION You do not see rooms any more. You see broadcast domains, and the
CONCLUSION walls between them are the only walls you actually respect. It has
CONCLUSION made you very good at this and very bad at being anywhere.
BEATS      6
RESEARCH   Empathy -1
BONUS      Interfacing 2
BONUS      Perception 1
BONUS      Shivers 1

# ===========================================================================
# THE TOOLKIT
# ===========================================================================

:: item field_laptop
NAME   The Field Laptop
DESC   Fourteen inch, matte, every sticker scraped off with a thumbnail so it
DESC   photographs as nobody's. The lid shuts on a folded antenna it should not
DESC   have room for. It boots to a login prompt that does not say whose.
EQUIP  yes
MOD    Interfacing 2
MOD    Encyclopedia 1
MOD    Composure -1

:: item wireshark_capture
NAME   A Capture You Should Not Have
DESC   Eleven minutes of the concourse network, timestamped, saved to disk
DESC   because you could not help yourself. Nine thousand frames. Somewhere in
DESC   there is the moment the station stopped, and it is going to be one
DESC   packet, and it is going to be small.
EQUIP  yes
MOD    Logic 1
MOD    Perception 1

:: item burp_ca
NAME   Your Own Certificate Authority
DESC   A CA certificate with your name on it, generated this morning, valid
DESC   until it is not. Nothing you proxy is readable until something at the
DESC   other end agrees to trust it. Getting things to trust it is most of the
DESC   job and all of the crime.
EQUIP  yes
MOD    Interfacing 1
MOD    Suggestion 1

:: item proxmark
NAME   The Card Reader That Reads Backwards
DESC   A Proxmark in a sunglasses case, coil taped where the hinge was. Hold it
DESC   near a transit card for four seconds and it comes away knowing enough to
DESC   be that card. It is the least deniable object you own.
EQUIP  yes
MOD    Hand Eye Coordination 1
MOD    Interfacing 1
MOD    Authority -1

:: item cloned_card
NAME   Somebody Else's Wednesday
DESC   A blank fob carrying a maintenance credential that belongs to a man who
DESC   is at this moment eating a sandwich on the westbound platform. It opens
DESC   the doors he is allowed through. It will be his name in the log, not
DESC   yours, and you have decided to think about that later.
EQUIP  no

:: item ducky
NAME   A USB Stick That Types
DESC   Grey, unbranded, deliberately boring. It is not storage. Plugged into
DESC   anything with a keyboard driver it introduces itself as a keyboard and
DESC   starts typing at nine hundred words a minute, and it does not care who
DESC   is watching the screen while it does.
EQUIP  yes
MOD    Hand Eye Coordination 1
MOD    Half Light 1

:: item gate_token
NAME   A Fare Token, Replayed
DESC   Forty-one bytes lifted off the turnstile handshake and written back down
DESC   unchanged. The gate has no way to know it has seen this before, which is
DESC   not a clever attack, it is an old one, and it is still here.
EQUIP  no

:: item attendant_lanyard
NAME   Nikolai's Lanyard
DESC   Station staff, westbound, twenty-two years on the network. He put it on
DESC   the counter to rub his eyes and did not put it back on. The photograph
DESC   is from before the beard.
EQUIP  yes
MOD    Authority 1

# ===========================================================================
# TASKS
# ===========================================================================

:: task get_out
TITLE  Get out of Voskresenskaya
DETAIL Every gate, shutter and service door in the station went closed at 02:14
DETAIL and stayed closed. You are inside a building that has decided nobody is
DETAIL leaving, and nobody has come to tell you why.

:: task why_it_sealed
TITLE  Find out what is holding the doors
DETAIL Nothing here has failed. The lights are on and the clock is right. Find
DETAIL the one process that stopped and would not let go.

:: task make_a_way
TITLE  Open something
DETAIL There are four ways out of this concourse and each of them wants a
DETAIL different thing from you. Pick one and be the person who can do it.
